invariant: INV-17
file: rsa_sign64.c
catches: test/lint-invariants.sh
reason: table_select writes zeros to its output in a loop of its own and
  then adds each entry under its mask, as its first form did. A compiler
  makes that loop a call to the C library's fill. The digit is computed
  before the call and read after it, so clang for arm64 keeps it in a
  register a callee saves, and the multiplication after the read saves
  that register in its frame: four bits of the private exponent in a
  dead frame, which no wipe names. bin/rsa_sign_equiv_test finds them
  under clang for arm64, as one byte that differs between the stacks two
  exponents leave. Under gcc, and under clang for x86-64, the digit sits
  in another register and that test passes, so it cannot hold the rule
  where CI compiles. inv-16-rsa-table-read refuses a write to the output
  that is not the select of an entry under its mask.

--- old
    for (uint64_t entry = 0; entry < TABLE_ENTRIES; entry++) {
        uint64_t differ = ((entry ^ index) + (TABLE_ENTRIES - 1)) >> WINDOW_BITS;
        *hidden = mask_of_bit(differ ^ 1);
        uint64_t mask = *hidden;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            o[i] = (o[i] & ~mask) | (table->powers[entry][i] & mask);
        }
    }
--- new
    for (size_t i = 0; i < k; i++) {
        o[i] = 0;
    }
    for (uint64_t entry = 0; entry < TABLE_ENTRIES; entry++) {
        uint64_t differ = ((entry ^ index) + (TABLE_ENTRIES - 1)) >> WINDOW_BITS;
        *hidden = mask_of_bit(differ ^ 1);
        uint64_t mask = *hidden;
        for (size_t i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            o[i] |= table->powers[entry][i] & mask;
        }
    }
